  is the only difference. The patched build passes the 112 tests of upstream test/corpus at v0.23.2.
patch: valid TypeScript that 0.23.2 parses as ERROR (O18), each change and where it comes from:
  - generic tagged template, sql<Row[]>`SELECT ...`: own; the tree upstream PRs #342/#350 expect,
    whose rule breaks `f<T>()`
  - `typeof import("x")` in a call's type arguments: own (type query wins over the call expression)
  - `import("x").T[]`: own
  - inline import member types in generic call arguments, including arrays and unions (issue #18):
    own. Two explicit conflicts retain both type and expression paths until the surrounding
    syntax decides. Generic calls keep their type_arguments; runtime import calls, indexed
    accesses and less-than comparisons retain their expression trees. Source bytes, UTF-16
    offsets and ERROR/MISSING handling are unchanged; malformed controls still fail closed.
  - bare `&` in JSX text: upstream PR #371 (open)
  - bare `&` in JSX attribute strings: own (scanner, reusing the character reference check of #371)
  - member `in?:` after a member without semicolon: own (scanner)
  - `a < -1, b`: own (the `-1` literal type no longer wins over the unary expression up front)
  - `i < unique.length`: own (`unique` joins the reserved identifiers)
  - newline before a generic type member: own (automatic semicolon insertion before `<`
    when no expression binary operator is valid). Generic expression continuations remain
    expressions; newline-separated type arguments rejected by TypeScript remain errors.
  - `export type * from` and `export type * as Name from`: own (type-only wildcard exports
    require a source; named type export clauses keep their optional source).
  - a nested generic function type as a call's first type argument, `f<<T>(value: T) => T>()`:
    own bounded external lookahead distinguishes its generic-function arrow from a shift
    expression. The token end stays after the first `<`; the grammar validates the full type.
    Direct calls and whitespace/newline continuations are covered. Lookahead has a 4096-step
    budget, handles quoted text/comments within generics/parameters, and does not claim full
    TypeScript equivalence or support for every intervening-trivia form.
  - literal NUL in strings, templates, regexes, comments and JSX text: own. The external scanner
    uses lexer->eof(lexer) instead of testing lookahead == 0; lexer.patch changes 26 generated
    negative character-class guards per dialect from lookahead != 0 to !eof. The pinned grammars
    do not explicitly exclude NUL in these classes. Raw input, token text and spans are preserved.
    See docs/research/typescript-nul-parser.md for reproduction and remaining syntax limits.
  - explicit type-parameter variance (`in`, `out`, `in out`): own, matching TypeScript 4.7's
    optional variance annotation syntax. The annotation precedes the named type parameter;
    constraints/defaults and const type parameters retain their existing shape. Both dialects
    have valid, malformed and import-location controls; the 112 upstream corpus tests pass.
  - contextual identifiers `using` and `abstract`: own. Both join the reserved-identifier
    alternatives so typed method parameters (including multiline signatures) and optional
    object properties keep identifier/property_identifier nodes. Existing `using` assignment
    and abstract class/member productions are unchanged; missing-type controls still error.
  - import type queries with type arguments (`import("m").Type<Args>`, `import("m").Ns.Type<Args>`,
    `import("m").Type<import("n").Other<Args>>`, `typeof import("m")`, `typeof import("m").Ns.Type<Args>`):
    own. The type-position import member accepts optional type arguments. A self-conflict keeps
    the member and its arguments until the closing `>` decides. The same forms parse in .ts, .d.ts
    and .tsx. Malformed type arguments still error.
  Not fixed: a generic tagged template after
  await or a prefix unary operator (-, !, typeof, void) parses as a call of that operator, as f<T>()
  already did in 0.23.2.
